Read-only AOT blocks with unaligned atomic faults are recorded and compiled with half barriers (FEX 3bec2ac, on top of 0029)

FEX compiles a 32-bit guest's loads and stores as acquire loads and release
stores (LDAPR, STLR) to keep x86's memory order. An iPad's CPU faults on
one that crosses 16 bytes. With the JIT, FEX handles the first fault by
patching the access into a plain one with a half barrier (LDR + DMB ISHLD,
DMB ISH + STR). A read-only AOT block is never patched, so the access is
emulated through a Mach exception every time it runs. NFSU2's widescreen
fix scans the game's memory byte by byte, and on the iPad the game spent
minutes in it and never opened its window:

  [mach_exc] UNHANDLED #5500 pc=0x1118e05d8 addr=0x700112c72d ...
  D 24 Handled unaligned atomic: new pc: 1118E05DC

- HandleUnalignedAccess calls ContextImpl::NoteUnalignedAOTBlock for a
  ReadOnlyCode block before it emulates the access. That logs the block
  once ("[aot-unaligned] block ... recorded for a half-barrier compile",
  for the first 64) and writes it to the code map with the new flag
  CodeMap::HalfBarrierTSOBlock (bit 31 of the block offset). The code map's
  summary line counts the faults.
- The offline compiler compiles a block with that flag (in place of its
  plain entry) with Context::SetCompileHalfBarrierTSO, and LoadMemTSO and
  StoreMemTSO then emit the patched form right away (LDUR + DMB ISHLD,
  DMB ISH + STUR, same size). It prints how many blocks of an image it
  compiled that way.

- The offline compiler takes back a library's ready code map, which has no
  executable marker (WriteNewCodeMap writes only its library load). Marking
  every ready map of a game with codemap.py halfbarrier made process-all die
  in Result.at(*ExecutableFileId) on the first library map.
- WINPAD_AOT_TRACE=1 makes the offline compiler name each block before it
  compiles it, to find a block whose compile never ends (the game's
  combase.dll took a whole 600 s batch).

